#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554713} :activity/id #uuid "ca108108-17d4-5f4b-8827-323beb81ff84" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168 :topic/id #uuid "e02cd9a7-60ac-54e8-865a-ffe6fe24b944" :topic/math-academy-id 369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acd652e-3691-5e84-bd19-a5ecf963137a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553891} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bfd8d1-d406-5259-8076-e675c9f53bf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d00df18b-1c5e-50a7-89f5-e1bc96838901" :question/math-academy-id "q-314883" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7de8ecd3d178cf07377aef5ca96ce28f638700e6284d151e3578ba11ed87327a.png)\n\nEthan notices a drone descending. He estimates that the distance between his location and the point on the ground directly beneath the drone is $150\\,\\text{ft}.$ If the angle of elevation $\\theta $ between him and the drone decreases at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{rad/s},$ at what rate is its altitude decreasing when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$ Assume that the rate of decreasing altitude is **positive** and do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/5024a642ac27698d848577165b08e3158d3248d50c064a79432fcad6e2df90d5.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone’s altitude, and $x$ is the distance between Ethan and the shadow. The quantity $x=150\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between Ethan and the shadow is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{150} \\\\ y & =150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.1$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150(4)(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of $60\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "3a9e38c3-b897-5f5d-93dc-fef0dc09243e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554678}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25909b6a-2bda-5136-9440-1e737f4b1063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}\\sin^2\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553935} :answer-field/id #uuid "075eec91-479f-514d-b496-401b7ceb517c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70ef2d3a-cac0-51c8-b6cc-9ac0c81e8893" :question/math-academy-id "q-343183" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c410aeaa3ceeafe3b072233a51020594370431e35244ca240637c5b124807983.png)\n\nA firefighter $F$ is lowering a rescue basket $B$ from a helicopter. The basket stays $40\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground while it moves horizontally toward the firefighter at a speed of $12\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the cable and the horizontal is increasing.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cf/cfac2040d258398133397c5322e650bf60f59c7c372c97bfecec654eb3a4a278.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the basket's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the firefighter and the basket. The quantity $y=40$ feet does not change with time because the basket height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{40}{x} \\\\ x & =40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,\\text{ft/s}$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{12}{40} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{40}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{rad/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "663845a1-8a70-5c83-82d2-15fcdebd3d49"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554708 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33182b33-0b36-5640-a4c9-778cbc8910ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.55\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553959} {:answer/id #uuid "913b0f46-1aad-5da8-b8dd-1bf4b4bb9711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.78\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554184} {:answer/id #uuid "b840f7bb-0678-5531-ad85-8cea6f5889e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.84\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554270} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8772f4-5572-5416-89ab-0f1ffa36973b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554322} {:answer/id #uuid "e24d129b-6aba-55f9-bf48-3093ebfec375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.93\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554322} :answer-field/id #uuid "c44dc184-d6a9-51f1-aaf9-4f2394dbe32b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "847493a1-e66a-5ae4-9cf4-e1e68a84c19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121713" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecb02db9984510219c6209392c9f35df52eca11dacc802a133aa51c4e5065ede.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and climbs with an angle of elevation of ${60}^{∘}$ and a vertical speed of $4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, calculate the actual speed of the drone in the direction it is traveling.\nRound your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69314419f6e0b93236fc048c6b9b6b262b09ff2cc061996353fbe23870a12726.png)\n\nLet $y$ denote the vertical distance from the drone's projection to the starting point, and $z$ be the distance between the drone and the starting point, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 4≈4.62\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the drone is approximately $4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9666dca7-9168-5a9d-9fb0-a3b4bc64ba0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fac3cfa-7abf-57a7-a5c4-1542f336991f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553956} {:answer/id #uuid "5f4dce0c-9b7e-5a89-af25-5aa2a3346d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554054} {:answer/id #uuid "6a41d46c-11a5-5084-856a-7e8059285981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "500\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554077} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45e093-51a8-53a6-b55a-12e8101873db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554384} {:answer/id #uuid "fe72755a-430b-5ef8-a7fc-ef1ec5c17011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554423}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554423} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75072c9-8631-52be-933c-279fbcae935a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5cbfe710-aab6-5e49-b65f-e20759621640" :question/math-academy-id "q-53189" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff93274daa7f179f86343e59b7cf277c0e70555fdfdd3cce113c3cba149b08ac.png)\n\nAn airplane $A$ is flying horizontally at a constant height of $800\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the airplane and a nearby radar station $R$ is decreasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the aircraft as a function of $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/29/29d06d071e6983d3487426b08d5a5548a611134dcf116674d199f6d3de03f246.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the airplane's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the radar station and the airplane. The quantity $y=800$ meters does not change with time because the airplane stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{800}{x} \\\\ x & =800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.05$ (negative because the angle is decreasing). Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(-0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the aircraft is $40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "9b218fda-b689-564e-9378-201209ba1bc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554568 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14580d19-87db-5577-b886-dede88420678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553909} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5b4088-b923-5d8d-b898-9ef038b7221e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} {:answer/id #uuid "78790129-6ad3-5b94-a1fd-e8e6c328e78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554111} {:answer/id #uuid "9212d90c-2509-5164-b39b-daaf88c41146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554190} {:answer/id #uuid "f407d38e-a26b-5b5c-8e63-0d3d2b384765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5096af22-5b72-55f8-89a3-f7336942ca60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4220ee9a-71ec-56c9-a4ec-d0480b90a44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-53199" :question/problem "![](images/06/06f965498f6415c08b32c5ae30235a8eec65d6dd67cd1f349e80bcbe88bd5495.png)\n\nA boy $B$ is flying a kite $K$ with a $20\\,\\text{m}$ length of string. If the kite is rising vertically at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ at what rate is the angle of elevation $\\theta $ increasing when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second."} :step/id #uuid "a88a66de-c5b3-5b9e-aac7-5154923ab26e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554708}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553859}]}
